(SAGE). American journal of epidemiology. 2017. Mar 15;185(6):414–28. doi: 10.1093/aje/kww125 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Pathak H. Effect of water borne diseases on indian economy: a cost-benefit analysis. An Rom Sov Ser Med Gen. 2015. Jun 1; 1:74–8. [ Google Scholar ] 42. Dhara VR, Schramm PJ, Luber G. Climate change & infectious diseases in India: Implications for health care providers. The Indian journal of medical research. 2013. Dec;138(6):847. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Syed MA, Alnuaimi AS, Zainel AJ, AA H. Prevalence of non-communicable diseases by age, gender and nationality in publicly funded primary care settings in Qatar. BMJ nutrition, prevention & health. 2019;2(1):20. doi: 10.1136/bmjnph-2018-000014 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Mohan V, Mathur P, Deepa R, Deepa M, Shukla DK, Menon GR, et al. Urban rural differences in prevalence of self-reported diabetes in India—The WHO–ICMR Indian NCD risk factor surveillance. Diabetes research and clinical practice. 2008. Apr 1;80(1):159–68. doi: 10.1016/j.diabres.2007.11.018 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Steyn K, Damasceno A. Lifestyle and related risk factors for chronic diseases. Disease and mortality in sub-Saharan Africa. 2006; 2:247–65. [ Google Scholar ] 46. Alaba O, Chola L. The social determinants of multimorbidity in South Africa. International journal for equity in health. 2013. Dec;12(1):1–0. doi: 10.1186/1475-9276-12-63 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Biswas T, Islam MS, Linton N, Rawal LB. Socio-economic inequality of chronic non-communicable diseases in Bangladesh. PloS one. 2016. Nov 30;11(11): e0167140.45. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0167140 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Di Cesare M, Khang YH, Asaria P, Blakely T, Cowan MJ, Farzadfar F, et al. Inequalities in non-communicable diseases and effective responses. The Lancet. 2013. Fe